findings.body:
The assertions in `testRequestAuthorization` were converted from `XCTAssertTrue` to Swift's `assert`. That is a regression in test quality for two reasons.

In Debug, a failed `assert` traps the whole test process, so the runner reports a crash rather than a failed test case. In optimized builds, `assert` is compiled out, so the closure's checks never run and the test passes regardless of behavior.

The mock completes synchronously, so the closure runs inside the test. The conversion is not needed to avoid a timing problem. The closure can keep the XCTest assertions if the mock is captured by value rather than through `self`, which is what the `[center]` capture was for.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/ClientTests/Helpers/NotificationManagerTests.swift" lines="26-29"}

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/firefox-ios-tests/Tests/ClientTests/Helpers/NotificationManagerTests.swift" lines="26-29"}
func testRequestAuthorization() {
    let center = self.center
    notificationManager.requestAuthorization { granted, _ in
        XCTAssertTrue(granted)
        XCTAssertTrue(center?.requestAuthorizationWasCalled ?? false)
    }
}

This keeps `XCTAssert*` semantics and avoids capturing `self` in a `@Sendable` closure.
